# The build context carries sources only. The build writes dist/ itself, and the
# settings, the log and the repository metadata have no business in an image.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
dist/
build/
build-*/
logs/
config.json
*.exe
Dockerfile
.dockerignore

# Nothing that is a key goes into an image, whatever it is called. The context
# is copied into a layer, and a layer is readable by anyone who can pull the
# image or read the build cache of the machine that made it: a signing key that
# reaches one signs releases every installed copy accepts. The COPY lines in
# the Dockerfile name what the build reads, so this is the second line of
# defence rather than the only one.
#
# The patterns carry "**/" and are not bare names, which matters: a pattern
# without a slash is matched against the name at the top of the context only,
# so "*.pem" left a key in internal/ sitting in the layer that copies internal/.
# Measured, not assumed: with the bare patterns a decoy internal/leaked.pem was
# present in the build stage, and with these it is not.
signing.key
**/*.key
**/*.pem
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
